WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.138
When several teams build a system at the same
time, agreeing on API behavior allows them to

00:00:05.138 --> 00:00:10.333
make progress before every implementation is
finished. A messaging client can initially call

00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:15.359
a mock inbox service that returns a fixed list
of messages in the agreed response format.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:20.637
The diagram shows the same client first
connected to a mock and later to the real
service

00:00:20.637 --> 00:00:25.659
through the same contract. This helps developers
build parsing, presentation, and basic

00:00:25.659 --> 00:00:30.853
error handling early. It does not prove end-to-
end correctness: a mock that always returns

00:00:30.853 --> 00:00:36.106
the same inbox will not reflect newly sent
messages, enforce permissions, or reproduce real

00:00:36.106 --> 00:00:38.241
persistence and concurrency behavior.

00:00:38.241 --> 00:00:43.865
The same backend contract can serve several
kinds of client. A mobile application, a desktop

00:00:43.865 --> 00:00:49.488
application, and a browser interface may all
fetch messages, send messages, and authenticate

00:00:49.488 --> 00:00:53.889
through the same service API while presenting
different user interfaces.

00:00:53.889 --> 00:00:59.334
In a traditional web design, a server builds
HTML; in a single-page design, browser code

00:00:59.334 --> 00:01:04.718
obtains data and updates the interface. The
diagram shows different presentation layers

00:01:04.718 --> 00:01:10.225
converging on the same domain operation. These
choices form a continuum, and a system can

00:01:10.225 --> 00:01:13.690
combine server-rendered pages with browser
interactions.

00:01:13.889 --> 00:01:19.004
Moving presentation to the browser does not move
the authority to approve operations there:

00:01:19.004 --> 00:01:21.590
the backend must still validate every request.
